1 INSTALL

   Makes images known to the system, and forgets them: the known file
   list.  An image installed with privileges runs with them, whoever
   runs it.

   Format

     INSTALL subcommand [file-spec]

2 Description

   The known file list holds the images INSTALL has made known.  An image
   installed /PRIVILEGED=(priv[,...]) runs with those privileges as its
   image privileges (F$GETJPI's IMAGPRIV), enabled in the process's
   current privileges (CURPRIV) while it runs, though the process isn't
   authorized for them; when the image exits they're gone, and the
   process has its permanent privileges again.  It's how a program a
   system manager trusts does, for any user, what needs a privilege
   that user hasn't got.  While it runs, $SETPRV may enable its
   privileges again after the program disabled them, but not
   permanently.

   An entry is for one version of one file on a mounted volume: the
   version found when it was installed.  A later version of the file
   runs without the entry's privileges until the old entry is removed
   and the new version installed.

   ADD and REMOVE need the CMKRNL privilege
   (%SYSTEM-F-NOCMKRNL); LIST needs none.  INSTALL's $STATUS is an
   image's, with the bit that says its message has been shown set
   (%X10000001 when it succeeds).  govax's known file list lasts as
   long as the session.

2 ADD

   Installs an image.  CREATE is the same subcommand.

   Format

     INSTALL ADD file-spec

   The file's type is .EXE by default.  A file already installed is
   %INSTALL-W-FAIL with -INSTALL-E-KFEXISTS.  Only /PRIVILEGED changes
   how govax runs the image; the other qualifiers are recorded and
   shown by LIST.

3 Qualifiers
/ACCOUNTING

      /[NO]ACCOUNTING

   Records that the image's runs are to be accounted for (Acnt).

/EXECUTE_ONLY

      /[NO]EXECUTE_ONLY

   Records that the image may be run with execute access alone (Exe).

/HEADER_RESIDENT

      /[NO]HEADER_RESIDENT

   Records that the image's header is to stay in memory (Hdr).

/OPEN

      /[NO]OPEN

   Records that the image's file is to stay open (Open).

/PRIVILEGED

      /PRIVILEGED=(priv-name[,...])

   The privileges the image runs with, by name (SYSNAM, OPER, ...), or
   ALL (Prv).

/PROTECTED

      /[NO]PROTECTED

   Records that the image is a protected one (Prot).

/PURGE

      /[NO]PURGE

   Accepted and recorded; not shown.

/RESIDENT

      /[NO]RESIDENT

   Records that the image's code is to stay in memory (Resid).

/SHARED

      /[NO]SHARED

   Records that the image's pages are to be shared (Shar).

/WRITEABLE

      /[NO]WRITEABLE

   Records that a shared image's writable pages are shared (Wrt).

2 LIST

   Shows the known file list, or the entry for one file.

   Format

     INSTALL LIST [file-spec]

   The entries are listed under a heading for each volume, directory,
   and type ("DISK$WORK:<WORK>.EXE"), each the file's name and version
   and the abbreviations of its attributes.  A file with no entry is
   %INSTALL-W-FAIL with -INSTALL-E-NOKFEFND.

3 Qualifiers
/FULL

   Shows each entry's access count (the times it has been run since
   it was installed), its open and shared counts, and its privileges.

2 REMOVE

   Forgets an image: it runs with no privileges of its own afterward.
   DELETE is the same subcommand.

   Format

     INSTALL REMOVE file-spec

2 Examples

   1.$ INSTALL ADD DUA1:[TOOLS]SETNAME/OPEN/PRIVILEGED=SYSNAM

     Lets every user run SETNAME, which puts names in the system
     logical name table, with SYSNAM.

   2.$ INSTALL LIST/FULL DUA1:[TOOLS]SETNAME

     DISK$WORK:<TOOLS>.EXE
        SETNAME;1        Open          Prv
             Entry access count         = 0
             Current / Maximum shared   = 1 / 0
             Privileges = SYSNAM

   3.$ INSTALL REMOVE DUA1:[TOOLS]SETNAME

     Takes SETNAME's entry, and its privileges, away.
